[Line "150 Attack: 5.Qd2 b5 6.f3 Bb7 7.O-O-O Nd7 8.h4 h6"]
[Section "4.Be3 a6: the 150 Attack"]
[Summary "White sets up Qd2, f3, long castling and a pawn storm. Black delays ...Ngf6 and short castling so that Bh6 has nothing to hit, kills the storm with ...h6 and strikes with ...Rc8 and ...c5."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 { White's most flexible move. The Modern is built on not showing the knights early, so Black
does not hurry either. } 3... d6 { [%cal Gd6e5] The standard order. With the pawn on d6 and the king's knight still at home,
e4-e5 has no target and only costs White time. }
( 3... Nf6?! { Natural, but it hands White a tempo-gaining pawn move. } 4. e5 Ng8 { White has gained two tempi with
the pawn and Black has a worse version of a Pirc. } )
4. Be3 { [%cal Gd1d2,Ge3h6] The 150 Attack. Qd2 and Bh6 aim to trade off Black's fianchettoed bishop, f3 and O-O-O
prepare the king, and h4-h5 comes against whatever Black has built. Against a Pirc set-up (...Nf6 and ...O-O) it is
the most dangerous system for Black, because the castled king and the knight on f6 are the targets. } 4... a6 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gb5b4,Gc8b7]
The Tiger's Modern, named by Hillarp Persson, with a logic borrowed from the Najdorf. ...a6 prepares ...b5 and ...b4, which
drives the c3-knight away from the defence of e4, and the bishop on b7 then hits the pawn. It also takes b5 away from White.
Above all it is a waiting move: Black commits his knights and his king only when the position has shown what White is doing. }
5. Qd2 b5 { [%cal Gb5b4,Rb4c3] } 6. f3 { Protects e4 against ...b4 and ...Bxe4, and prepares g4 and Nge2 or Nh3. }
6... Bb7 7. O-O-O Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gc7c5,Gg8f6] The queen's knight goes to d7, not the king's knight to f6. From d7 it supports
...c5 and ...e5. The king's knight stays at home until the d4-pawn has been exchanged: then e4-e5 is no longer a lever and Bh6
no longer hits a castled king. With the king on e8, Bh6 Bxh6 Qxh6 only trades a pair of bishops and puts the white queen on the edge of
the board. }
( 7... Ngf6?! { Too early. Now e4-e5 comes with tempo, and with ...O-O to follow the Bh6 and h4-h5 attack is on. } )
8. h4 { [%cal Gh4h5,Rh5g6] White starts the storm before Black has done anything: h5 and hxg6 would open the h-file. }
( 8. Bh6 Bxh6 9. Qxh6 Ngf6 { [%cal Gf6e4] Nothing has been won by the trade. The queen on h6 stops short castling, but it is far from the
centre, Black develops with a threat on e4, and the king is not going to castle short in the next moves anyway. Equal. } )
( 8. g4 h6 9. h4 { Transposes to the main line. } )
8... h6 { [%cal Rh4h5,Gg6g5] The right reply. The pawn takes h6 away from the bishop (Bh6 is gone for good) and g5 from the knight
(Ng5 is gone too), and if White plays h5 the answer ...g5 locks the wing. White has to find his play in the centre or on the c-file,
where Black is ready. }
( 8... h5!? { [%cal Gh7h5] The other way to stop h5. It leaves g5 as a hole for a white knight, which is the main line of the
next game, where the same structure arises from the move order 7.h4. } 9. Nh3 Rc8 )
9. Kb1 { Takes the king off the c-file, which is about to open, and guards a2 and c2. Other tries: 9.Nh3 Rc8 10.g4 c5 (Black's move order
is the same) or 9.g4 Rc8. } 9... Rc8 { [%cal Gc8c5,Gc7c5] The rook belongs on the c-file for the break that is coming: after ...c5 and dxc5 the knight
recaptures with a gain of time (it hits e4) and the rook already stands behind it. } 10. g4 { White gains space on the
kingside, but he has now spent several moves on the wing where his own king is not. }
10... c5 { [%cal Gc5d4,Gb7e4] The break. Whatever White does about the pawn on d4, Black gets the e5-square under control and can then
bring out the king's knight without fear of e5. } 11. dxc5 { [%cal Rd2d7] } 11... Nxc5 { [%cal Gc5e4,Gb7e4] Two pieces now hit e4.
White's king's bishop is still at home and the b2-b3 and Nge2 moves needed to cover everything cost time. } 12. Bd4 { Hits the knight and
the bishop on g7 at once. } ( 12. Nh3 Qa5 { Pressure on c3 and a2, and ...b4 comes next. } ) 12... Bxd4 { Black takes the bishop that
White would like to keep, so that the knight on c5 cannot be harassed and the d4-square belongs to nobody. } 13. Qxd4 { The knight on c5 is
defended by the rook on c8. Black continues with ...Ngf6, ...Qc7 or ...e5, depending on the move. The position is balanced: White has
the more advanced pawns, Black has the more active pieces and the open c-file, and his king, still in the centre, is in no danger. } *

[Line "Critical test: 7.h4 h5 8.Nh3 Nd7 9.Ng5, the knight on g5"]
[Section "4.Be3 a6: the 150 Attack"]
[Summary "White plays h4 before castling, Black stops h5 with ...h5, and the white knight lands on g5. Black ignores it, plays ...Rc8 and ...c5 and gets sharp counterplay."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 5. Qd2 b5 6. f3 Bb7 7. h4 { Before castling. Black cannot allow h5 for free. } 7... h5 { [%cal Rh4h5]
The pawn stops h5 for good. The cost is the g5-square, which a white knight can use and which Black cannot control with a pawn. This is
the critical test of 7...h5, because a knight on g5 cannot be challenged by a pawn. }
( 7... h6 { [%cal Gh6h5] The Semi-Hippo way. Without the g5-hole for a knight, the pawn on h6 meets h5 with ...g5 and the wing is locked.
It is also good, and the safer choice if the sharp knight line below does not appeal; the price is that White has gained space with h4-h5 and
the pawn on g5 can become a target for f3-f4. } 8. h5 g5 )
8. Nh3 { [%cal Gh3g5] The knight heads for g5, where it is safe from pawns and hits e6 and f7. It does not block the f-pawn. } 8... Nd7 9. Ng5 { [%cal Rg5f7,Rg5e6] } 9... Rc8 { [%cal Gc7c5] Black does not chase the knight
(there is no good way to). He lets it stand where it is and starts the counter-break; the knight on g5 is far from d4 and the centre. }
10. O-O-O c5 { [%cal Gc5d4,Gb7e4] } 11. dxc5 { 11.Kb1 is the other main choice and also met by ...cxd4. } 11... Nxc5 { [%cal Gc5e4,Gb7e4,Gc5a4] }
12. Bd4 { The bishop takes the best diagonal and pins down the g7-bishop's activity. } 12... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4,Gf6g4] The king's knight can finally
come out: after ...Nxc5 the e5-lever is gone, and from f6 it adds to the pressure on e4 and will let the king castle. } 13. Kb1 O-O { Castling is possible
now: the pawn on h5 keeps the h-file shut and the knight on g5 has nothing to bite on. }
14. g3 { A useful waiting move: it covers f4 and h4 and prepares Bg2. } 14... e5 { [%cal Ge5d4] The central break comes with a threat to the bishop on d4. } 15. Be3 b4
{ [%cal Gb4c3,Gc5e4] Now c3 is under attack and the e4-pawn is overloaded. } 16. Ne2 Ncxe4!? { [%cal Ge4d2,Ge4g5] The point of the whole sequence.
After the exchange the knight on e4 attacks both the queen and the knight on g5. } 17. fxe4 Nxe4 { The knight forks Qd2 and Ng5. The study continues with
18.Qxb4 Qc7 and finds strong compensation for Black. The sequence is forcing and double-edged, so it should be checked at the board before it is relied on. A calmer
path is 14...Qc7 (the move most often played) with a normal game. } *

[Line "Quiet 6.Bd3: 6...Bb7 7.Nf3 Nd7 8.O-O c5"]
[Section "4.Be3 a6: the 150 Attack"]
[Summary "White protects e4 with the bishop and develops normally. Black treats it as a Najdorf-type position and strikes at once with ...c5, before White's king is safe."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 5. Qd2 b5 6. Bd3 { Defends e4 with the bishop, which makes ...b4 harmless. Against it, Black develops the
same pieces and prepares the same break. } 6... Bb7 { [%cal Gb7e4] } 7. Nf3 Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gc7c5] The knight prepares ...c5 and keeps the other
knight at home. Moving it to f6 now would run into e5. } 8. O-O c5! { [%cal Gc5d4] Hit the centre before the white pieces are placed. If White waits
(9.Rfe1 cxd4 10.Nxd4 Ngf6 11.f3 O-O), Black has a good version of the Dragon: the knights are developed, the king is safe and the long diagonal is
open. }
9. dxc5 { The most common reply. White opens the d-file and wants to harass the knight. } ( 9. Rfd1 Qc7! { Stops the e5 trick and prepares
...cxd4. } ( 9... Ngf6? { Natural, but it walks into the pin on the d-file. } 10. dxc5 dxc5 11. e5 Ng4 12. e6! { Black loses material: the knight on d7
is attacked and the queen on d2 stands on the same file as the rook on d1, so ...Qxd2 does not save it. } ) )
9... Nxc5 { [%cal Gc5d3,Gc5e4] } 10. b4 { Wins a tempo on the knight but loosens the queen's wing. } 10... Nxd3 11. cxd3 Ngf6 { The bishop pair
is gone, but White's pawn structure has holes on b4 and d3, and the pressure on e4 is back with tempo. Black has a comfortable game: he
castles, plays ...O-O, ...Rc8 and ...Qb6 or ...Nd5 depending on the move. Equal. } *

[Line "5.h4: 5...h5 and the Semi-Hippo 5...h6"]
[Section "4.Be3 a6: the 150 Attack"]
[Summary "White starts the h-pawn storm at move five. Black stops it with ...h5, and if White goes for Nh3-g5 he trades the knight with ...Bxh3."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 5. h4 { White starts the storm before developing. The point is that ...Nf6 now runs into h5. }
5... h5 { [%cal Gh7h5] Stops the pawn. It is the same idea as in the previous game, and the structure often comes out the same way. }
( 5... h6!? { Semi-Hippo. If White plays h5 Black answers ...g5 and has a very solid wall. After 6.Qd2 b5 the play is like the
main line with the h-pawns locked. } 6. Qd2 b5 )
6. Nh3 { [%cal Gh3g5] The knight prepares Ng5. } ( 6. Qd2 b5 7. f3 Bb7 { Transposes to the previous game. } )
6... Bxh3 { [%cal Gc8h3] Black swaps off the knight before it reaches g5. It gives up the bishop pair, but White's rook on h3 is badly placed,
the h-pawn is stuck and Black's structure has no weaknesses. } 7. Rxh3 { [%cal Rh3h5] } 
7... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4,Gf6g4] } ( 7... Nd7 { Playable too. } ) 8. f3 { Defends e4 and covers g4. } 8... Nbd7 { Black has completed his development
and can still choose between castling and keeping the king in the centre. The game is balanced; the typical plan is ...c5 or ...b5 and
...Qc7 followed by long-term pressure on e4. } *
